\begin{proof}
Take $G=C_2\times C_2=\{e,a,b,ab\}$, where $a^2=b^2=e$ and $ab=ba$. Let $H=\{e,a\}$. Because $G$ is abelian, every subgroup is normal, so $H\trianglelefteq G$.

Define $\sigma:G\to G$ by $\sigma(e)=e$, $\sigma(a)=b$, $\sigma(b)=a$, and $\sigma(ab)=ab$. The images of the generators $a$ and $b$ again satisfy the defining relations of $C_2\times C_2$, hence $\sigma$ is an automorphism. But $\sigma(H)=\{e,b\}\ne H$. Therefore $H$ is not characteristic in $G$, while it is normal in $G$.
\end{proof}
